---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { findInsight } from '../../insights';

const insight = findInsight('audit-trail-as-a-design-requirement')!;
---

<Article
  insight={insight}
  standfirst="If you can only reconstruct how a figure was derived by asking the person who made it, you do not have an audit trail. You have a dependency."
>
  <p>
    "Audit trail" usually appears in a feature list, somewhere near the bottom,
    described as a report you can run. That framing is the problem. A trail you
    have to reconstruct on request is not a trail — it is an investigation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Lineage is either a property of how figures are produced, or it does not
    really exist. It cannot be added afterwards, because the information needed
    to establish it was discarded at the moment the figure was created.
  </p>

  <h2>The test</h2>

  <p>
    There is a simple test for whether a finance system has real lineage. Pick a
    figure in a filed return or a signed statement. Then, without speaking to
    anyone, answer three questions:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Which ledger entries make up this figure?</li>
    <li>What adjustments were applied to it, and on what basis?</li>
    <li>Who approved it, when, and what exact version did they approve?</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Most finance functions cannot answer all three for an arbitrary figure from
    two years ago. Many cannot answer the first one for last quarter. The
    knowledge exists, but it exists in a person, a workbook and an email thread —
    three places, none of which is the accounts.
  </p>

  <h2>Why "we can rebuild it if asked" is not good enough</h2>

  <p>
    The usual objection is that the information can be reassembled if somebody
    genuinely needs it. Technically true, and it misses three costs.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>It arrives at the worst moment.</strong> The request comes from an
    auditor under time pressure, or from a tax authority with a deadline
    attached. Reconstruction happens when it is most expensive and least
    convenient.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>It decays.</strong> Reconstruction depends on memory and on files
    still being where somebody left them. Both degrade. At eighteen months the
    reconstruction is plausible; at three years it is often no longer possible at
    all.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>It is a single point of failure with legs.</strong> If one person can
    reconstruct the reasoning and nobody else can, that person's departure is a
    control failure. This is the risk finance teams most consistently
    under-price, because the person is usually excellent and has never let anyone
    down.
  </p>

  <h2>What it means to design for it</h2>

  <p>
    Treating lineage as a design requirement changes specific decisions. Four
    that matter:
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Figures are derived, not entered.</strong> If a number is typed into
    a return, its derivation is gone. If it is computed from a mapped ledger, the
    derivation is inherent — the system knows what it summed because it did the
    summing.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Adjustments are objects, not amounts.</strong> An add-back should
    carry its reason, its basis of measurement, and a link to the evidence. Stored
    as a bare number in a cell, it is an assertion. Stored as a record, it is
    support.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Periods lock.</strong> Once something is filed, the underlying data
    must be frozen. Without a lock, a later edit silently changes what was
    reported and the trail now describes something that never happened. With one,
    corrections become explicit prior-period adjustments — visible, dated,
    attributable.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Approval captures a version.</strong> "Approved by the director"
    means little if the document changed afterwards. Sign-off should reference the
    exact state approved, so a subsequent change requires a new approval instead
    of inheriting the old one.
  </p>

  <h2>The unexpected benefit</h2>

  <p>
    Teams adopt this expecting it to help with audit and enquiry. It does. But
    the benefit that shows up first is internal: reviews get faster.
  </p>

  <p>
    A reviewer's real job is deciding whether a figure is reasonable. When
    lineage is available on the figure itself, that decision can be made
    directly. When it is not, the reviewer spends most of their time
    reconstructing what was done before they can form a view — which is why
    review is so often the longest step in a close and the one that scales worst.
  </p>

  <p>
    Build the trail into how figures are made, and you get the defensibility as
    an eventual insurance policy and the review speed as an immediate dividend.
    The insurance is what gets budgeted; the dividend is what people notice.
  </p>
</Article>
